<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kateri Tekakwitha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1656">1656</a>–<a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kateri Tekakwitha was a Mohawk-Algonquin woman born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> mission of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Known for her strict piety and penances, she died at the age of 24. She became the subject of devotion across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and in 2012 she was canonised as the first Native American saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kateri-tekakwitha.jpg" alt="Kateri Tekakwitha" width="320" height="534" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Father Claude Chauchetière, S.J. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CatherinaeTekakwithaVirginis1690.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kateri Tekakwitha was born around 1656 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> village of Ossernenon, in what is now northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Her name, Tekakwitha, means she who bumps into things in Mohawk. Her father, Kenneronkwa, was a Mohawk chief, and her mother, Kahenta, was an Algonquin woman who had been captured in a raid, adopted into the tribe and earlier baptised by French missionaries. When Tekakwitha was around four, her parents and brother died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>. She survived with facial scars and impaired eyesight and went to live with her father's sister and her husband, a chief of the Turtle Clan.</p><p>In 1666 the French burned the three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> villages, and the peace that followed required the Mohawks to tolerate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries. At eleven she met the Jesuits Jacques Frémin, Jacques Bruyas and Jean Pierron. Pressed to think of marriage around thirteen, she refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the spring of 1674 she told the Jesuit Jacques de Lamberville of her wish to become a Christian. He baptised her on Easter Sunday, 18 April 1676, giving her the name Catherine, of which Kateri is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawk</a> form. Some Mohawks opposed her conversion and accused her of sorcery, and others stoned and harassed her. She fled about 200 miles to the Christian mission of Sault Saint-Louis, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, and joined it in 1677.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a> she practised severe penances, which some priests opposed because of her poor health. With her friend Marie Thérèse Tegaianguenta she tried to found a Native religious order, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> rejected. In 1679, on the Feast of the Annunciation, she consecrated herself to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as her husband.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tekakwitha died on Holy Wednesday, 17 April 1680, in the arms of Marie-Thérèse. The Jesuits Claude Chauchetière and Pierre Cholenec wrote her biographies in 1695 and 1696. She is often called the Lily of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohawk-people">Mohawks</a>.</p><p>The cause for her canonisation was begun by American Catholics at the Third Plenary Council of Baltimore in 1885, and some 906 Native Americans signed letters supporting it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> beatified her on 22 June 1980 and Benedict XVI canonised her on 21 October 2012, the first Native American woman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> to be canonised.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kateri Tekakwitha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After European arrival some Haudenosaunee became Christians, among them <b>Kateri Tekakwitha</b>, the first Native American saint.</blockquote><a href="/culture/haudenosaunee">Haudenosaunee · Beliefs &amp; Worldview</a></li><li><blockquote>Some became Christians, among them the Mohawk-Algonquin <b>Kateri Tekakwitha</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/haudenosaunee">Social Hierarchy of the Haudenosaunee</a></li><li><blockquote>Jesuit missions drew Catholic converts north, among them <b>Kateri Tekakwitha</b>, to Kahnawake near Montreal.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Montgomery County, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahnawake">Kahnawake</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.91,-74.44&amp;year=1671" aria-label="Montgomery County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2752 640 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Kateri Tekakwitha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3202 910h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3202 910h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.91,-74.44&amp;year=1671">What was here in 1671? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
